problems.problem:
10.6. Synchro-eclipse

As is known, the Moon recedes from the Earth by 3.8 cm per year on average. Because of this, in some epoch in the future an interesting situation may arise: exactly every draconic year (the period of return of the Sun to one definite node of the lunar orbit in the sky) a central solar eclipse will occur, visible on the Earth at the zenith.

1) Determine when this can happen for the first time in the future;

2) For this moment find the greatest phase of this central eclipse;

3) Determine how many other solar eclipses will be observed during the draconic year between these central eclipses, and what their greatest phases will be.

Consider the orbits of the Earth and the Moon to be circular, the recession of the Moon from the Earth to be uniform, and the inclination of its orbit to the ecliptic, the distance from the Earth to the Sun, and also the radii of the Sun, the Earth and the Moon to be constant. The angular velocity of the motion of the nodes of the lunar orbit is $\omega_N = -C\cdot\Omega^2/\omega$, where $\Omega$ and $\omega$ are the angular velocities of the orbital motion of the Earth around the Sun and of the Moon around the Earth respectively, and $C$ is a constant.

